## INTRODUCTION
Porous solid catalysts, as represented by zeolites which have been widely used as solid acid catalysts,² are ofgreat importance for the chemical and petroleum industries.3 Continuous efforts from both academia and industry have been devoted to the search for new types  of  porous  materials  as  environmentally benign heterogeneous catalysts particularly for fixed bed reactions.4 Among various candidates explored over the past two decades, porous metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are placed in a unique position due to their structural versatility, high surface areas, tunable pore sizes, and tailorable catalytically active sites.9 MOFs have recently been investigated as solid acid catalysts (either Lewis acid orBronsted acid) to catalyze a variety of reactions under batch conditions,° but the utilization of them as heterogeneous catalysts for fixed bed reactions where zeolites have played a major role has been muchless exploited.' This could be due to the relatively weak activity of individual active sites in MOFs thereby resulting in poor catalytic perfo rmances.Therefore, the development of strategies to enhance the catalytic activity of MOFs as solid acid catalysts for fixed bed reactions is highly needed.
It's been reported in homogeneous systems that Bronsted acid and Lewis acid can mutually promote each other when combined due to the synergistic effect thereby enhancing the catalytic activity.1² Inspired by those investigations1² and to achieve high activity for MOFs as heterogeneous solid acid catalysts, we report herein a strategy of combining Bronsted acid MOF framework with Lewis acid centers to form the Lewis acid@Bronsted acid MOF (Figure la).As a result of the synergyl3 between Bronsted acid sites and Lewis acid centers,² the resultant Lewis acid@Bronsted acid MOF demonstrates excellent catalytic performances in a series of fixed bed reactions, outperforming two benchmark zeolite solid acid catalysts of H-Beta and HMOR in terms of both activity and selectivity.
