## RESULT AND DISCUSSION
Materials preparation, physiochemical characterization, and local structure analysis. To illustrate our strategy, we chose MIL-101-Cr $\mathrm{\left(Cr_{3}(F)(H_{2}O)_{2}O[(O_{2}C)C_{6}H_{4}(C O_{2})]_{3}\right)^{14}}$ asthe platform material considering its high water/chemical stability and readiness for fun ctionalization via either postsynthetic modification (PSM)15 or pre-synthesis'% methods. The Bronsted acid functionalized MOF, MIL-101- $\mathrm{CrSO_{3}H}$ , was first prepared according to the procedures reported in literature.l0g Given its strong Lewis acidity, Al(III) center was incorporated into MIL$101\mathrm{-Cr-SO_{3}H}$ (Bronsted acid MOF framework) by reacting $\mathrm{\AlCl_{3}}$ with MIL-101-Cr $\mathrm{SO_{3}H}$ in ethanol solution followed by water treatment to affordMIL-101-Cr-SO3H·Al(III)）(Lewis acid@Bronsted acid MOF) (Scheme S2).
Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD） studies suggest that MIL$101{\cdot}\mathrm{Cr}{\cdot}\mathrm{SO_{3}H}{\cdot}\mathrm{Al}$ (III) retains its structural integrity during the synthesis process, as evidenced by the consistence in PXRD patterns between MIL-101-Cr-SO3H:Al(III) and the pristine MIL$101\mathrm{-Cr-SO_{3}H}$ (Figure S3). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy · shows an aluminum signal at a $75.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Figure S4) associated with the peak of $\mathsf{A l2p},^{17}$ indicating the presence of Al(III) in $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101{\cdot}\mathrm{Cr}{\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}{\cdot}\mathrm{Al}(\mathrm{III}),$ .TEM-EDS analysis (Figure S5)indicates the Al(III) is uniform distributed in MIL-101-Cr SO3H·Al(II). ICP-MS and elemental analysis indicate the ratio of $\mathrm{Cr/Al}$ in MIL-101-Cr- $\mathrm{SO_{3}H}$ Al(III) are ${\sim}1/2$ ， corresponding to two Al atoms per $S\mathrm{O}_{3}^{-}$ group. In addition, the ion chromatograph analysis indicates almost all chloride atoms convert to hydroxyl groups under water treatment with only a trace amount of chloride ions in the MIL-101-Cr-SO3HAl(II) sample. $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ sorption isotherms (Figure 1） show a decrease in BET surface area and pore volume, from $1571~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $0.686\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ to $1449{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}$ and $0.605\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively after theintroduction of Al(III) centers.
