Catalytic performance investigation. To evaluate the performance of MIL-10l-Cr-SO3HAl(II) as a heterogeneous solid acid catalyst, on a fixed bed reactor at a reaction temperature of $393\mathrm{~K~}$ and ambient pressure, we conducted the benzylation reaction of aromatic hydrocarbons (e.g., mesitylene) with benzyl alcohol (BA), which is an important reaction to produce pharmaceutical intermediates and fine chemicals'9; as well as being environmentally benign with water as a byproduct,20. For the benzylation of mesitylene with BA (Scheme 2) as shown in Figure 3, MIL-10l-Cr-SO3HAl(III) gives a remarkable increase in catalytic performance during the reaction process and reaches a maximum value of conversion after $120~\mathrm{{min}}$ with an exceptional conversion of BA (approaching $100\%$ ) and high selectivity $(>99\%)$ ofthe target product 2-(benzyl)-1,3,5-trimethyl-benzene(BTMB). It outperformstwo benchmark zeolites, mordenite $(\mathrm{HMOR})^{20c}$ and H-Beta.21 which have beenwidely investigated for benzylation reactions, as well as three MOFs, HKUST-1, $^{22}\mathrm{MIL}{-}100{-}\mathrm{Fe},^{23}$ and $\mathrm{PW}@\mathrm{MIL}.101.\mathrm{Cr}^{11\mathrm{d}}$ under the same reaction conditions. Compared  with $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101{\cdot}\mathrm{Cr}{\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}{\cdot}\mathrm{Al}(\mathrm{III}) $ ， the two zeolite catalysts show alower conversion that decreases with reaction time and a consistently lower selectivity (Figure 3b). The maximum BA conversion for HMOR and H-Beta is $84.1\%$ and $87.8\%$ ， respectively. The three MOF catalysts exhibit very low conversion with values peaking ${<}20\%$ . The catalytic performance of MIL-101- $\mathrm{Cr-SO_{3}H}$ Al(II) also exceeds that of $\mathrm{\AlCl_{3}}$ a commonly used Lewis acid catalyst for the benzylation of aromatic compoundsl9 (Figure S7). To the best of our knowledge, both the activity and the selectivity of MIL-101-Cr-SO $_\mathrm{3H}$ :Al(II) for the benzylation of mesitylene with benzyl alcohol are among the highest values reported for porous materials based solid acid catalysts.2021 It's worth noting that no loss of activity was observed over a $6\mathrm{~h~}$ reaction period, indicating $\mathrm{MIL-101\mathrm{-}C r\mathrm{-}S O_{3}H\cdot A l(I I I)}$ retains the high activity and selectivity over extended reaction times; this is in striking contrast with HMOR and H-Beta, which start to lose the activity within 1 h andbecome almost totally inactive in less than 5 h.
In order to further assess the merits of the catalysts, we calculated the yield according to the experimental conversion and selectivity (Figure 4a). MIL-101-Cr-SO3HAl(III) demonstrates an increasing yield with the reaction time and reaches a yield of ${\tt>}99\%$ after $^{2\mathrm{{h}}}.$ In contrast, a steady decrease in yield over the reaction time was observed for all other catalysts. The two zeolites, HMOR and H-Beta exhibit a decline in yield with a value ofless than $50\%$ after $3\ \mathrm{~h~}$ . The three MOFs, HKUST-1, MIL-100-Fe and PW@MIL-101-Crshow even muchlower yields of ${<}20\%,$ ,and lose nearly all their activity after $3\mathrm{h}.$ In addition,in terms of the amount of product produced during the reaction time period from $30\mathrm{min}$ to $240\mathrm{min}$ (Figure 4b), MIL-101-Cr-SO3HAl(III) far exceeds all other catalysts, with the yield product amount as 1.7, 2.4, 53, 15, and 85 times as that produced by HMOR, H-Beta, HKUST-1, MIL-100-Fe, and PW@MIL-101-Cr, respectively. Such exceptional catalytic performance of MIL-101-Cr-SO3H Al(III) in comparison with HMOR, H-Beta, and other materials was also observed when benzene and p-xylene were used as the reactants in the fixed bed reactionsfor the benzylation ofaromatic hydrocarbons with benzyl alcohol (Scheme S3, S4, Figure S8, S9).
Investigation of synergy effect between Bronsted Acid Framework and Lewis Acid Center. We reason that the excellent catalytic performances of $\mathrm{MIL-101-Cr-SO_{3}H\cdot A l(I I I)}$ result from the synergistic effect of Bronsted acid framework and Al(III) Lewis acid center. Control experiments were performed on MIL-101-Cr, $\mathrm{\bfAl_{2}O_{3}}$ and MI $_{\mathrm{L-101-Cr-SO_{3}H}}$ in the context of benzylation of mesitylene with benzyl alcohol under the same rea ction con ditions. MIL-10l-Cr shows a very low conversion (Figure S9a), meaning a negligible contribution from the open metal sites of the framework to the high catalytic activity of MIL-101-Cr $\mathrm{SO_{3}H}$ Al(III). Both $\mathrm{\bfAl_{2}O_{3}}$ and MIL-10l-Cr-SO3H exhibit a depression in catalytic activity and selectivity when compared to MIL-101-Cr$S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ -Al(III) (Figure S1O). The yield of BTMB catalyzed by $\mathrm{\bfAl_{2}O_{3}}$ or MIL $-101{\mathrm{-Cr-SO_{3}H}}$ is significantly lower than that catalyzed by $\mathrm{ML}\mathrm{-}101\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Cr}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{Al}(\mathrm{III})$ (Figure Sa), highlighting that Lewis acid@Bronsted acid MOF can more effectively activate aromatic hydrocarbons bonds when compared to the respective parent materials. Control experiments were also performed on the mixture of $\mathrm{\bfAl_{2}O_{3}}$ and $\mathrm{MIL-101-Cr-SO_{3}H,}$ whose catalytic performances are also lower than MIL-101-Cr-SO3HAl(II). These results further suggest that the higher catalytic activity ofMIL-l0l-Cr$\mathrm{SO_{3}H\cdot A l(I I I)}$ result from the synergistic efect of combining Bronsted acid MOF framework with Al(II) Lewis acid centers, thereby highlighting the advantages of Lewis acid@Bronsted acid MOF for fixed bed reactions. These results have also been supported by the product amount yielded via different catalysts during the reaction period from $30\mathrm{min}$ to $120\mathrm{min}$ (Figure 5b).
## CONCLUSION
In summary, we have illustrated a strategy to obtain MOFs as heterogeneous  solid acid catalysts with higher activity by combining Bronsted acid MOF framework with Lewis acid centers to afford Lewis acid@Bronsted acid MOF, as exemplified in the context of MIL-101-Cr-SO $_\mathrm{3H}$ Al(III). Asaresult of the synergy between Bronsted acid sites and Al(II) Lewis acid center, MIL$101{-}\mathrm{Cr}{\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}{\cdot}t$ Al(III）demonstrates excellentcatalytic performances as heterogeneous solid acid catalyst in the fixed bed reactions for benzylation of aromatic hydrocarbons with benzyl alcohol, outperforming some benchmark zeolite solid acid catalysts. Our work thereby not only provides a new approach to preparing highly active MOF-based catalysts, but also lays a solid foundation for developing MOFs as a new type of highly efficient heterogeneous catalysts for fixed bed reactions. The employment of MIL-10l-Cr-SOH-Al(II) as heterogeneous solid acid catalyst for other types of reactions as well as the development of other MOFs as highly efficient heterogeneous catalysts is currently under way in our laboratory.
## EXPERIMENTALSECTION
Synthesis of MIL-101-Cr-SO3H. MIL-101-Cr-SO3H was synthesized according to the procedure reported by the litera ture.10g
Synthesis of MIL-101-Cr-SO3H·Al(III). $500.0\mathrm{mg}$ freshly prepared MIL-1Ol-Cr-SO3H sample was first heated at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 48 h on the degasing port of the Micrometrics ASAP 2020 Surface Area and Porosity Analyzer with a dynamic vacuum of $_{<2}$ micron torr. The degassed sample was moved into a heavy-wall pressure tube in a glove box under the ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere. Subsequently,300.0 mg anhydrous $\mathrm{\AlCl_{3}}$ $(>99\%$ purity, Fisher Scientific) was added into the tube, followed by $100.0~\mathrm{ml}$ anhydrous ethanol $(>99.5\%$ purity, Sigma-Aldrich). The sealed tube was then heated at $90^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ to afford the product of MIL-101 $\mathrm{Cr-SO_{3}H}$ Al(III), which was filtrated and washed by methanol $(>99\%$ purity, Sigma-Aldrich) to remove excess AlCl followed by further washing with water and methanol. The filtered sample of MIL-101-Cr-SOHAl(III) was dried under dynamic vacuum on the Schlenk lineat $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ ICP analysis data: Cr: $12.49\%$ ; Al: $13.01\%$ ； Elemental analysis data: C: $23.08\%$ ;H: $0.96\%$ ; S: $7.71\%$ ; Ion chromatograph data: Cl: $<0.25\%$
XANES experiments. All the Al K-edge X-ray absorption near edge structure (XANES) data was collectedat the spherical grating monochromator (SGM) beamline at the Canadian Light Source (Saskatoon, Canada).24 The energy scale of the Al K-edge spectra were calibrated using AlPO4 assuming a value of $1566.1\mathrm{eV}$ for the major peak.25 The photon energy resolution was about $0.1\ \mathrm{eV}.$ .The fine powder of the MIL-101-Cr-SO $_\mathrm{3H}$ -Al(III) and reference materials was spread on double-sided carbon tape on a Cu sample holder. The partial fluorescence yield (PFY) was measured using an Amptec silicon drift detector (SDD) with an energy resolution of $\displaystyle{\sim}150\ \mathrm{eV}.$ . The Al spectra were normalized by simultaneously measuring the incident flux (Io) from an Au mesh ( $90\%$ transmission) placed before the sample.
Catalytic experiments. To evaluate the catalytic performance of these catalysts, the benzylation of aromatic hydrocarbons with benzyl alcohol was first chosen at a reaction temperature of 393 K on a fixed-bed reactor. Before beingloaded into the fixed-bed reactor, the MOF samples were activated in vacuum at $393\ \mathrm{K},$ and zeolites were calcined at 823 K in the air. The catalysts (MIL-101- Cr-SO3H·Al(III), HMOR, H-Beta, HKUST-1, MIL-100-Fe) $\mathrm{PW}@\mathrm{MIL}.101.\mathrm{Cr},$ AND AlCl) $\left(50.0\mathrm{mg}\right)$ ）were mixed with $1.0\mathrm{g}$ silica sand and then loaded into the fixed-bed reactor. The bed temperature was kept at 393 K for one hour in the flow of nitrogen thereafter aromatic compounds and benzyl alcohol with a molar ratio 80:1 were introduced into the reactor at $0.06\mathrm{ml}$ I/minunder a nitrogen flow of $10\mathrm{ml/min};$ the reaction were monitored every 30 min. For control experiments, the amounts for catalysts of MIL 101-Cr $\mathrm{{Al}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{3},$ MIL-101-Cr-SO3H were $50.0\mathrm{mg}$ each; those for the mixture of Al ${}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ and MIL-101-Cr- $S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ were $15.0\mathrm{mg}$ and $35.0\mathrm{mg},$ respectively.
